  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA similar setup to Tuesday night is on tap for Wednesday night \nwith high pressure parked overhead. Light to calm wind and clear\nskies overnight will allow for efficient radiational cooling \nand fog development in river valleys. Like Tuesday night, don't \nthink that it leaks out of the valleys very much, but cold \nsurfaces and bridges within river valleys could become slick in \nthe morning. Fog will dissipate after sunrise as temperatures \nbegin to rise.\n\nThe expansive surface high pressure dominates through the rest of \nthe week and the first half of this coming weekend with a bone dry \ncolumn. This will allow for diurnal mixing to erode a few degrees \noff of dew points in the afternoons. Temperatures will gradually \nmoderate back into the 60s for most of the area by the end of \nthe week when the surface high slides east and promotes \nsoutherly gradient flow. Probability for 60 degree highs reaches \n70-90% for most of the area Thursday and Friday with areas to \nthe north of I-80 and along the Pennsylvania ridgeline. We will \ncontinue to see some decent diurnal temperature swings through \nThursday night as light wind and clearing skies allow for \nefficient radiational cooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur next chance for rain is still on track to arrive late this \ncoming weekend as a trough arrives from the west. Previously \nmentioned uncertainty still hasn't quite resolved itself as we await \nthe parent trough to eject from the Rockies. Depth of the trough \nvaries with a deeper one introducing higher rain chances and a less \namplified solution with lower rain chances. There are two\npotential upstream features that could impact the system's\ntrack: 1) the parent trough ejecting out of the Rockies and 2) \na shortwave sliding down through Manitoba. A weaker embedded \nshortwave on the leading edge of the ejecting trough would lend \nto faster longwave movement and lesser rain chance locally. A \nstronger embedded shortwave in Manitoba could then phase with \nthe parent CONUS trough and lend to higher rain chance locally.\nThe outcome with the shortwave out of Manitoba currently looks to\nbe the likeliest outcome, however, the drier solution could \nstill pan out. \n\nDespite the very dry antecedent atmosphere in place, \nPWATs will recover back to around 0.75 and likely vary between\n0.50\" on the lower end and 1\" on the higher end. The chance for\nmeasurable rain rises to 40-60% areawide, but NBM 90th \npercentile, or the wet scenario, paints mainly 0.3-0.5\" for most\nwith accumulation between 0.60-1\" along the southwestern PA and\nnorthern WV ridgeline. Overall, this next system doesn't look \nto be much more than a wetting rain.",
